+//! AD-HOC: The spec leaves where databases live to the user agent. Ours live in the browser process, which keeps them
+//! for the session (on disk, unless the session is private) and shares them between processes; see
+//! LibWebCommon/IndexedDB/PersistedDatabase.h. Each process keeps working on its own in-memory copy: it loads a
+//! storage key's databases from the browser process once, sends it what each transaction it commits changed, and is
+//! sent what other processes committed. LibWeb/IndexedDB/Persistence.cpp is the bridge to the page client, and the
+//! databases and changes cross it as bytes, in the layout described there.
